  "title": "Brain-wide human sEEG during aversive eyepuffs and ketamine infusion (Kauvar, Richman, Liu et al. 2025; DANDI 001347)",
  "description": "Stereo-EEG recordings from seven adult epilepsy patients undergoing extended clinical monitoring at Stanford Hospital, collected during brief aversive eyepuffs and a 40-minute ketamine infusion (0.5 mg/kg). The dataset is a BIDS-iEEG derivative of the NWB series released on DANDI (001347). Each recording is a 20-minute window downsampled to 200 Hz, with channel-level anatomical labels but no electrode coordinates. It supports study of brain-wide emotional responses to sensory experience and their modulation by a dissociative drug.",
  "methods_description": "Clinical depth electrodes (AdTech, platinum contacts) were implanted for epilepsy monitoring and recorded with a Nihon Kohden JE-120 amplifier. The signal was anti-aliasing low-pass filtered (FIR) and downsampled to 200 Hz, and cut into 20-minute windows around a series of aversive eyepuffs (10 min before and after puff start). The NWB data were converted to BrainVision float32 in microvolts, scaled by the authors' scale_to_microvolts factor, with no further filtering, resampling, or re-referencing.",
